In Java, converting an object to a Data Transfer Object (DTO) is a common task in applications, especially when you need to transfer data between layers or services. While Spring provides utilities for this, you can also implement it manually. Below is an explanation of both approaches.

---

## **1. Manual Object to DTO Conversion (Without Using Spring)**

When not using Spring, you can manually map the fields from the entity to the DTO.

### **Example:**

Let's say you have an `Employee` entity and a `EmployeeDTO`.

#### **Entity:**
```java
public class Employee {
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;
    private LocalDate hireDate;

    // Getters and setters
}
```

#### **DTO:**
```java
public class EmployeeDTO {
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;

    // Getters and setters
}
```

#### **Manual Conversion:**
```java
public class EmployeeMapper {
    public static EmployeeDTO toDTO(Employee employee) {
        EmployeeDTO dto = new EmployeeDTO();
        dto.setId(employee.getId());
        dto.setName(employee.getName());
        dto.setEmail(employee.getEmail());
        return dto;
    }
}
```

### **Usage:**
```java
Employee employee = new Employee();
employee.setId(1L);
employee.setName("John");
employee.setEmail("john@example.com");

EmployeeDTO dto = EmployeeMapper.toDTO(employee);
```

